      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting points $A$ and $B$, we can use the distance formula for 3D space. \n\nFor 2-D:\\[\\huge{\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2}=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)(x_2-x_1) + (y_2-y_1)(y_2-y_1)}=\\sqrt{x_1^2 + y_1^2 - 2x_1x_2 - 2y_1y_2 + x_2^2 + y_2^2}=\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2 + (y_2-y_1)^2}}\\]\n\nTo 3-D:\\[\\huge{\\sqrt {(x_3 - x_1)^2 + (y_3 - y_1)^2 + (z_3 - z_1)^2} =\\sqrt{(x_3-x_1)^2 + (y_3-y_1)^2 - 2(x_3-x_1)(x_3-x_1)-2(y_3-y_1)(y_3-y_1)+ (z_3-z_1)^2 + (z_3-z_1)^2}=\\sqrt{(x_3-x_1)^2 + (y_3-y_1)^2 + (z_3-z_1)^2}}\\]\n\nIn this case, we have a right triangular prism and we know the length (10 inches) of two sides and the area of the base (20 x 10 = 200 square inches). To find the third side, (the leg connecting the two different lengths), use the formula for herons area: $s = \\frac{a+b+c}{2} \\rightarrow \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}$. Let $2x$ mean the length of the three legs connecting the cubes together. So use IBIS: $\\frac{200 + 2x + 2x}{2} \\rightarrow \\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}$ and then solve for $x$. In this case, the length is $12\\sqrt {13}$ inches. The answer is 12\\sqrt {13}",
